(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What underlies xerosis, and who is most affected?","choices":["Impaired stratum-corneum hydration promoted by ageing, low humidity, hot water, detergents, atopy and systemic disease, commonly affecting older adults especially in winter","Overgrowth of Malassezia in sebum-rich areas, in young men","Chronic venous hypertension in the lower legs, in older adults","An autoimmune process affecting the epithelial basement membrane, occurring most often in the elderly and remitting spontaneously after five or six years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, causing tightness, pruritus, rough scale, fissuring or eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 152"},{"q":"Which nail changes are seen in psoriasis?","choices":["Koilonychia and Beau's lines","Terry's nails and clubbing","Green nail discoloration and chronic paronychia of the nail folds","Onycholysis, pitting, splinter haemorrhages and subungual hyperkeratosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — these can occur with any variant but are common in psoriatic arthritis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 161"},{"q":"Why are the inguinal folds generally spared in irritant diaper dermatitis?","choices":["They are less exposed to urine and faeces","They have thicker skin than the surrounding area","They are protected by resident Candida","They receive less friction from the diaper"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the eruption follows exposure, so it lands on convex surfaces. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which adverse effects follow prolonged topical corticosteroid use?","choices":["Hyperpigmentation and hypertrichosis","Atrophy, striae, telangiectasia and hypopigmentation","Photosensitivity, dryness and marked irritation","Nephrotoxicity and hepatotoxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — which is why potency is matched to site and severity. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How is a poorly demarcated lesion described?","choices":["Present in more than one body region","Raised above the surrounding skin surface","Irregular or blotchy in appearance, without well-defined borders","Smaller than one centimetre in every dimension measured"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — as against a well demarcated lesion, which has clearly defined borders. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does this lecture say about a patient presenting with purpura?","choices":["It requires only outpatient dermatology follow-up","It is diagnostic of thrombocytopenia","It is a medical emergency until proven otherwise","It is a benign finding common in the elderly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lecture flags it explicitly rather than treating it as one finding among many. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does a Wood's lamp emit, and what is it used to evaluate?","choices":["Infrared light, used to assess perfusion","Short-wave ultraviolet light, used to sterilise the skin surface before a procedure is performed","Long-wave ultraviolet light, used to evaluate pigment changes in selected fungal or bacterial infections","Polarised white light, used to magnify pigmented lesions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a handheld device highlighting subtle changes in skin, scalp and hair. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of alopecia areata, and why is the hair loss non-permanent?","choices":["Repeated traction pulls the follicle from the scalp","Autoreactive T cells infiltrate the follicle and switch it from growing to resting, without destroying the stem cell compartment","Autoantibodies destroy the hair follicle and its entire stem cell compartment, so that any regrowth is never possible","Dihydrotestosterone progressively miniaturises the follicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the stem cells survive, so regrowth remains possible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 137"},{"q":"What distinguishes a plaque from a patch?","choices":["A plaque is always poorly demarcated while a patch is always well demarcated","A plaque is elevated and plateau-like; a patch is flat with no elevation","A plaque is larger than a patch","A plaque is fluid-filled and a patch is solid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both exceed one centimetre, so elevation is the discriminator. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the central principle of managing lichen simplex chronicus?","choices":["Systemic antibiotics given long term in order to prevent recurrent secondary infection","Excision of the affected plaque","Break the itch-scratch cycle, since recurrence is common unless the initiating itch is controlled","Long-term high-potency topical steroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — through education, trigger treatment, emollients, behavioural substitution, nail care and safe occlusion. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"How does guttate psoriasis present, and what precedes it?","choices":["Silvery plaques on the extensor elbows and knees","Erythematous patches with margins studded with subcorneal pustules, appearing typically during the third trimester of pregnancy","Raindrop-shaped erythematous papules and plaques in children, typically 2 to 3 weeks after a streptococcal infection or upper respiratory infection","Sudden generalised pustules with fever over the trunk and extremities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It often needs no treatment, though phototherapy and topical steroids may be used. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"Where do pemphigus vulgaris lesions typically begin, and in which populations is it more common?","choices":["On the lower legs in the gaiter region, and it is far more common in older men than women","On the scalp, and it is more common in children","In the axillae, and it is endemic in rural Brazil","In the mouth, and it is more common in Jewish populations and people of Mediterranean descent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus foliaceus by contrast is endemic in rural Brazil and rarely involves mucosa. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"Why does the medication history ask when each drug was started in relation to the rash?","choices":["Because the timing between starting a drug and the eruption is what implicates it","Because most drug eruptions occur only after six months of continuous therapy","Because only prescription medications can cause eruptions","Because the dose determines whether a reaction is possible"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a new rash after starting a medication, especially with fever or mucosal sores, is an urgent warning sign. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which genetic marker is seen in 90% of early-onset psoriasis?","choices":["HLA-B27","PSORS1","HLA-DR4","HLA-Cw6"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and in 50% of late-onset cases. PSORS1 is the major susceptibility locus; HLA-B27 associates with psoriatic arthritis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"What distinguishes a pustule from a vesicle?","choices":["A pustule contains leukocytes; a vesicle contains clear fluid","A pustule is larger than one centimetre and a vesicle is smaller","A pustule is solid and a vesicle is fluid-filled","A pustule sits in the dermis and a vesicle in the epidermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the contents, not the size, separate them. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are the four Ps of lichen planus?","choices":["Painful, pale, punched-out papules","Pruritic, pustular, patchy plaques","Purple, painless, peripheral pustular lesions","Pruritic, purple, polygonal, papules or plaques"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — flat-topped, pink to violaceous and shiny, coalescing into larger plaques. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"Which patients with lichen planus are at increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Those with cutaneous disease of the wrists and ankles only","Those with nail involvement","Those with hepatitis C","Those with genital and erosive oral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an important reason to identify and follow mucosal involvement. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"Which treatment is contraindicated in pregnancy for pustular psoriasis, and what is used instead?","choices":["Phototherapy is contraindicated in pregnancy; acitretin is used instead","Biologics are first line in pregnancy","Acitretin is contraindicated; high-potency topical steroids are used if pregnant","Methotrexate is safe in pregnancy and is preferred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 164"},{"q":"What is the main differential for nummular eczema, and what distinguishes them?","choices":["Tinea corporis — tinea has central clearing and nummular eczema does not","Psoriasis — psoriasis has silvery scale and nummular eczema does not","Pityriasis rosea — pityriasis has a herald patch","Contact dermatitis — contact dermatitis is well demarcated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and this single feature is the discriminator the lecture gives. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"How does eczema change in appearance from acute to chronic?","choices":["Acute shows pigment loss; chronic shows vesiculation","Acute shows erythema, oedema, papules, vesicles, oozing and crusting; chronic shows xerosis, fissuring, lichenification and pigment alteration","Acute disease shows lichenification and pigment alteration; chronic disease shows erythema, oedema, vesicles, oozing and crusting","The appearance is fixed and does not evolve over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with a subacute stage of scaling, erythema, papules and excoriations between them. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is finasteride, and what are its cautions?","choices":["A topical 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor applied to the crown, whose effects on libido and erectile function are permanent and do not reverse at all when the drug is stopped","An oral 5-alpha-reductase type 2 inhibitor; about 2% of men report reduced libido and erectile function, reversible on stopping, and it is not indicated in women and contraindicated in pregnancy","A topical vasodilator effective mainly at the crown","An oral antiandrogen used first line in women"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it works best combined with minoxidil. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"How do urushiol lesions present, and over what time course?","choices":["Well-demarcated plaques shaped like the object contacted, erupting within minutes of exposure and clearing within a day","Diffuse urticarial wheals resolving within 24 hours","Greasy scaling patches in sebum-rich areas over several weeks","Intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions, erupting within 4 to 96 hours and persisting up to 3 weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the linear pattern comes from grazing the plant in passing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"How does plaque psoriasis classically present?","choices":["Poorly demarcated plaques in the flexures","Erythematous to salmon pink papules and plaques with silvery scale on extensor elbows and knees, trunk, scalp, umbilicus, sacrum, and the tip of the penis and scrotum","Greasy yellow scaling in sebum-rich areas","Coin-shaped, intensely pruritic light pink scaly plaques scattered over the extremities and the trunk, uniform in appearance and without any degree of central clearing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with Koebner's phenomenon and the Auspitz sign. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"What is the atopic triad, and what proportion of patients have at least one of its components?","choices":["Asthma, allergic rhinitis and chronic sinusitis — approximately 60%","Atopic dermatitis, asthma and allergic rhinitis — approximately 80%","Atopic dermatitis, urticaria and food allergy — approximately 50%","Eczema, psoriasis and seborrhoeic dermatitis — approximately 30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How is mild perioral dermatitis treated once the corticosteroid is stopped?","choices":["A higher-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily","Topical ketoconazole","Oral prednisone","Topical metronidazole, erythromycin, pimecrolimus or azelaic acid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with oral tetracycline or doxycycline for more extensive or persistent disease. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"How does the distribution of atopic dermatitis change with age?","choices":["The distribution is the same at every age","Infants: palms and soles. Children: trunk. Adults: lower legs","Infants: cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces. Children: antecubital and popliteal fossae, wrists and ankles. Adults: flexures, hands, neck, face and eyelids","Infants: flexures and popliteal fossae. Children: extensor surfaces and scalp. Adults: cheeks, scalp and the extensor surfaces of the limbs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the shift from extensor to flexural is the pattern to hold. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which biopsy takes the whole lesion with deep and wide margins?","choices":["Excisional biopsy","Punch biopsy","Shave biopsy","Incisional biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it always needs stitches to close. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the timing of irritant contact dermatitis vary with the irritant?","choices":["Timing does not vary with the irritant","Mild irritants such as soaps may progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present within minutes or be delayed up to 24 hours or more","All irritants produce symptoms within minutes of exposure","All irritants produce symptoms 48 to 72 hours after exposure regardless of whether the agent is a mild soap or a concentrated acid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the agent determines the tempo. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What underlies stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Chronic venous hypertension, most commonly from chronic venous insufficiency","Arterial insufficiency arising from established peripheral arterial disease","Lymphatic obstruction","Autoimmune damage to the basement membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — incompetent valves, obstruction or impaired calf-muscle pumping raise venous pressure. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"What pattern does the pityriasis rosea eruption follow, and over what course?","choices":["It follows cleavage lines on the trunk in a Christmas tree pattern, spreading top down over days and fading over 4 to 6 weeks","It follows a dermatome and resolves within two weeks","It follows flexural creases and persists for months","It appears on the extensor surfaces of the limbs and persists indefinitely unless it is treated with phototherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The herald patch clears centrally leaving a cigarette-paper or collarette of scale. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 169"}]};
